10 REM hello-basic.bas
20 REM A minimal BASIC program. The line numbers are the program.
30 REM Run with: bas hello-basic.bas  (or any BASIC interpreter)
40 REM
50 REM Dartmouth BASIC, 1964: Kemeny and Kurtz.
60 REM This program would run on a Commodore 64 with no changes.
70 REM It would run on a TRS-80. It would run on the first IBM PC.
80 REM The line numbers are not decoration. They ARE the structure.
90 REM GOTO is not a bad habit here. It is the only branching mechanism.
100 PRINT "Hello, World"
110 END
